    {
      "paperId": "/article/10.1007/s12040-024-02504-y",
      "doi": "10.1007/s12040-024-02504-y",
      "title": "Thermal evolution of an evolving ensemble of accreting planetesimals on a planetary body and collision induced fragmented debris",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "S Sahijpal"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Thermal evolutionary models of an evolving ensemble of planetesimals and collision induced fragments of sizes 4–240 km, gradually accreting or fragmenting on a planetary body of sizes 100–6000 km, are presented. This is the first such model that integrates the thermal evolution of the planetesimals prior to their accretion on the planetary bodies and, subsequently, the collision induced fragments and their possible accretion on another planetary body. These numerical models based on a three-dimensional (3D) finite difference method have been developed to understand the thermal evolution of planetesimals, planetary embryos, and terrestrial planets that might have experienced complex evolution of gradual accretion, collision induced fragmentation, and re-accretion during the early stages of the evolution of the solar system over the initial ~10 million years (Myrs). The 3D models provide an advantage over the traditional 1D models in terms of the incorporation of the thermal evolution of accreting planetesimals and collision induced fragments along with the evolution of the associated planetary body. The radiogenic nuclide, 26Al, heating, along with the impact induced heating during the accretion of planetary bodies, were considered as the heat source. The principal aim is to understand the influence of these dynamical processes on the origin of chondrites, achondrites, and other planetary bodies in a generic manner. A novel numerical approach has been developed in a temporal manner to integrate the detailed thermal evolution of (i) the planetesimals accreting on a planetary body, (ii) the planetary body, (iii) the individual collision induced fragments (debris) of varied sizes, and (iv) the gradual accretion of the debris to form planetary bodies of successive generation. This is achieved by formulating a periodic surface boundary condition for defining planetary surfaces in a 3D spatial grid array that, over the passage of time, represents either (a) an ensemble of individual planetesimals, (b) an accreting planetary body or (c) collision induced fragments. The periodic boundary condition is systematically imposed in the array in a manner to simulate the planetesimals, the planetary body, and the collisional debris. A set of models with a range of distinct debris sizes has been developed. We could also numerically simulate the thermal evolution of a prolonged accretion growth of the planetary embryos over timescales of 5–10 Myrs in order to understand the relevance of prolonged accretion of these bodies on their planetary scale differentiation. We demonstrate that in case the onset of the accretion of the planetary body and the accreting planetesimals initiate within the initial 1.5 Myrs, the prolonged accretion of the planetary embryos over ~10 Myrs can still result in planetary scale differentiation even with a 0.1 fraction thermal energy retention of the impact induced energy released during accretion.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "Thermal evolutionary models of an evolving ensemble of planetesimals and collision induced fragments of sizes 4–240 km, gradually accreting or...",
